## Proof

**Setup.** Since $n!$ contains every prime $p \leq n$ as a divisor, the radical of $n!$ is the product of all primes up to $n$:
$$\operatorname{rad}(n!) = \prod_{p \leq n} p.$$
Hence $\log \operatorname{rad}(n!) = \sum_{p \leq n} \log p =: \theta(n)$, the Chebyshev theta function. The trivial bound $\operatorname{rad}(n!) \leq n!$ gives $\theta(n) \leq \log(n!) \leq n\log n$. We establish a linear bound.

**Key lemma.** For every integer $m \geq 1$,
$$\prod_{m < p \leq 2m} p \;\leq\; \binom{2m}{m} \;\leq\; 4^{m}.$$

*Proof of lemma.* If $p$ is a prime with $m < p \leq 2m$, then $p \mid (2m)!$ but $p \nmid m!$ (since $p > m$). Therefore $p$ divides $\binom{2m}{m} = \frac{(2m)!}{(m!)^2}$. As distinct primes are coprime, the product of all such $p$ divides $\binom{2m}{m}$, giving the first inequality. The second follows from $\binom{2m}{m} \leq \sum_{j=0}^{2m}\binom{2m}{j} = 2^{2m} = 4^m$. $\square$

Taking logarithms of the lemma yields, for all $m \geq 1$,
$$\theta(2m) - \theta(m) \;\leq\; 2m\log 2. \tag{$\ast$}$$

**Dyadic summation.** Let $n \geq 2$ and set $k = \lceil \log_2 n \rceil$, so that $2^{k-1} < n \leq 2^k$. Telescoping $(\ast)$ with $m = 1, 2, 4, \dots, 2^{k-1}$:
$$\theta(2^k) = \theta(1) + \sum_{j=0}^{k-1}\bigl[\theta(2^{j+1}) - \theta(2^j)\bigr] \;\leq\; \sum_{j=0}^{k-1} 2^{j+1}\log 2 = 2\log 2\,(2^k - 1) < 2^{k+1}\log 2.$$
Since $\theta$ is non-decreasing and $n \leq 2^k$,
$$\theta(n) \;\leq\; \theta(2^k) \;<\; 2^{k+1}\log 2 \;\leq\; 4n\log 2.$$

**Conclusion.** For every integer $n > 1$,
$$\boxed{\log \operatorname{rad}(n!) \;=\; \theta(n) \;\leq\; 4n\log 2 \;\approx\; 2.77\,n.}$$

This is $O(n)$, asymptotically better than the trivial $n\log n$. Concretely, $4\log 2 \approx 2.77 < \log n$ for all $n \geq 17$, so the bound $4n\log 2$ improves on $n\log n$ for every $n \geq 17$ (and is already better for smaller $n$ as well, since $\theta(n) \leq 4n\log 2 < n\log n$ whenever $n > 16$).

